    "q-61770": {
      "dom_id": "step-q61770",
      "name": "Question 17",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14187307/assets/7c7e161603d7f4dc59a8d19c4b28326232779c20fc6e029d168ad15e84661aa1.png)\n\nDavid's bag has green and yellow balls, in total there are $40$ balls. The ratio of green balls to yellow balls is shown in the diagram above. How many green balls are there?",
      "worked_solution": "The tape diagram shows $3$ boxes corresponding to green, and $5$ boxes corresponding to yellow. Therefore, the ratio of green balls to yellow balls in David's bag is $3:5.$\n\nWe are told that David's bag has $40$ balls in total. The tape diagram has $3+5=8$ boxes in total. Therefore, each box in the diagram above represents $40÷8=5$ balls in the bag.\n\nNow, we fill the number $5$ in each box of both tapes:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14187307/assets/8fdf2dfff7c145ae3774ec869a54752f2fb1c47ad98da3a21b45c36c3e1a9b09.png)\n\nSince there are $3$ boxes corresponding to green balls, and each box represents $5$ balls, the number of green balls must be:\n\n$$\n3\\times 5=15.\n$$\n\nSo, there are $15$ green balls in David's bag.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
