    "q-92809": {
      "dom_id": "step-q92809",
      "name": "Question 19",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14187307/assets/9b349ac221e04877ef3ad0474d9ddb1e95e7f01748f0be9975e18b9195090f5e.png)\n\nMs. Robinson's class has $32$ students. The ratio of boys to girls  is shown in the diagram above. How many girls are in Ms. Robinson's class?",
      "worked_solution": "The tape diagram shows $3$ boxes corresponding to boys, and $5$ boxes corresponding to girls. Therefore, the ratio of boys to girls born is $3:5.$\n\nWe are told that Ms. Robinson's class has $32$ students in total. The tape diagram has $3+5=8$ boxes in total. Therefore, each box in the diagram above represents $32÷8=4$ students in Ms. Robinson's class. \n\nNow, we fill the number $4$ in each box of both tapes:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14187307/assets/66ba3f4ea51eb34cf4c1f313058e56523236ebd36fcd092082b9f6ca63261d1b.png)\n\nSince there are $5$ boxes corresponding to girls, and each box represents $4$ students, the number of girls in Mr. Robinson's class must be:\n\n$$\n5\\times 4=20.\n$$\n\nSo, there are $20$  girls in Mr. Robinson's class.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
